  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1146 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nDry and unseasonably warm conditions will continue through the \nweekend. Earlier today an upper level ridge extended from near \nthe four corners region into the Pacific northwest with a \nnorthwest flow aloft present across the Central Plains. A weak \ncold front extended from west to east along the Nebraska/Kansas \nstate line. Models today, much like yesterday, continue to \nforecast the weak cold front moving into southwest Kansas \nthrough early tonight where it will briefly become stationary \nbefore lifting back north northeast during the day on Thursday. \nLittle in the way of cooler air is forecast to filter into \nportions of north central Kansas behind this front and based on\nthis plus that this front will move back northeast as a warm\nfront during the day on Thursday there will be little change in\nthe what to expect for high temperatures between this afternoon\nand Thursday afternoon. As for temperatures from Friday through\nthe weekend period the 850mb temperatures trends continue to \nindicate little change between 00z and 00z each day so afternoon\nhighs in the 80s can be expected to continue as the \nnorthwesterly flow aloft becomes more westerly. \n\nAfternoon wind gust over 40 mph possible this weekend. \nNorthwesterly flow aloft will back to the southwest over the \nweekend as the upper level ridge over the western United States \nweakens and shift east as our next upper level trough moves \nsouth from the Pacific Northwest on Friday into California and \nNevada on Sunday. In response to this upper trough establishing\nitself over the western United states this weekend, a trough of\nlow pressure at the surface over eastern Colorado will deepen \nand produce a tightening surface pressure gradient across \nwestern Kansas. Also a low level jet will develop over western \nKansas Friday and Saturday night with the 850 mb ensemble mean \nwind speeds forecast to range from 40 to 45 knots. These \nstronger winds mixing down to the surface during the day \ncombined with the tightening surface pressure gradient not only\ncontinues to support a increasing winds over the weekend but \nalso suggests that the NBM wind forecast may be a little low. \nThese conditions favor winds closer to the 75th percentile which\nis 25 to 35 mph with gusts in excess of 40 mph. \n\nThe chance for precipitation will return to southwest Kansas \nnext week along with cooler temperatures. A pattern transition \nwill be occurring late this weekend/early next week as the upper\nlevel ridge over the western United States today gets replaced \nby a deepening upper level trough/low by late Sunday/early \nMonday. Despite some ongoing timing/evolution issues that far \nout in the forecast period with this developing western United \nStates upper trough/low, the latest ensemble clusters indicate \nincreasing confidence that the on a deeper/slower solution may \nend up being more correct. Currently 60% of the ensemble cluster\nhas the upper level trough/low over California/Nevada early \nnext week. Given this there still appears to be a chance (low as\nit may be) for precipitation ahead of this system early in the \nweek as the southwesterly flow develops, however the better \nopportunity for an accumulating rainfall will be around the \nmiddle of the next week as a cold front drops south into the \nCentral Plains and the upper low/trough move towards the Plains.\nAs for temperatures next week...there will be a period of \nunseasonably cool temperatures following this cold front, \nespecially if we have cloud/precipitation during the day. Much \nlike yesterday the probability for highs from Tuesday through \nThursday to be less than 70s degrees ranges from 40 to 50 \npercent north and west of Dodge City.",
